From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 24 Aug 2026 02:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 38/38] fat: align the cache buffers so the card can DMA into
 them

These buffers are what the SD driver is handed, and on this machine it can DMA
straight into them -- but only if they are 32-byte aligned, because the cache
invalidate after a DMA read works on whole lines and an unaligned buffer takes
its neighbours' dirty lines with it.

They never are. The data starts immediately after the header inside one
AllocVec, so its alignment is whatever sizeof(struct BlockRange) leaves.

Measured under ISSUE-0051, with counters in our own SDHOST driver: of 509
transfers, 505 were rejected for alignment and went through a bounce buffer
instead. That copy is a plain word loop -- there is no NEON on m68k -- and
every one of its 2048 iterations per 8 KB is JITted. Over 4 MB the DMA engine
itself costs 79 ms and the bounce copy costs 4420 ms.

Thirty-one bytes of slack per buffer buys all of them the direct path.
---
--- a/rom/filesys/fat/cache.c
+++ b/rom/filesys/fat/cache.c
@@ -113,12 +113,31 @@
 
         for(i = 0; i < block_count && success; i++)
         {
-            b = AllocVec(sizeof(struct BlockRange)
+            /*
+             * Room to push the data past a 32-byte boundary.
+             *
+             * These buffers are what the SD driver is handed, and it can DMA
+             * straight into them -- but only if they are 32-byte aligned,
+             * because the cache invalidate that follows a DMA read works on
+             * whole cache lines and an unaligned buffer would take its
+             * neighbours' dirty lines with it.
+             *
+             * They never are: the data starts immediately after the header
+             * inside one AllocVec, so its alignment is whatever
+             * sizeof(struct BlockRange) leaves. Measured under ISSUE-0051,
+             * 505 transfers out of 509 were rejected for alignment and copied
+             * through a bounce buffer instead, and that copy is a JITted word
+             * loop costing fifty-five times the DMA it replaces.
+             *
+             * Thirty-one bytes buys all of them the direct path.
+             */
+            b = AllocVec(sizeof(struct BlockRange) + 31
                 + (c->block_size << RANGE_SHIFT), MEMF_PUBLIC);
             b->use_count = 0;
             b->state = BS_EMPTY;
             b->num = 0;
-            b->data = (UBYTE *)b + sizeof(struct BlockRange);
+            b->data = (UBYTE *)((((IPTR)b + sizeof(struct BlockRange)) + 31)
+                & ~(IPTR)31);
 
             if(b != NULL)
                 c->blocks[i] = b;
